= Astronomy
{wiki}

Astronomy studies celestial objects and phenomena, including <stars>, <planets> and <galaxies>. <Astrometry> measures their <positions> and motion; <astrophysics> uses <physics> to explain their properties and behaviour.

Much of <astronomy> begins with incoming <light>. An image tells us which direction the <light> arrived from, rather than directly providing the source's three-dimensional <position>. Two <stars> close together on the sky may be very far apart in space. The <celestial sphere> organizes these viewing directions; locating an object on it is a different task from measuring how far away that object is. Repeated observations turn this limitation into an opportunity: the changing direction of <light> contains evidence about both <Euclidean distance> and motion.

The particularly satisfying combination is <stellar parallax> with <proper motion>. The first supplies a geometric <Euclidean distance>, while the second measures how rapidly the viewing direction changes after accounting for the observer's annual motion. Together they give a transverse <speed>, connecting tiny <angles> on an image to kilometres travelled each second. This bridge from a flat sky map to moving <stars> is one reason <astrometry> matters to the study of the <Milky Way>.

= Astronomical model
{parent=Astronomy}

A representation used to predict or explain the apparent arrangements and <motion> of celestial objects. Agreement with a limited <observation> set need not identify a unique physical arrangement.

= Geocentric model
{parent=Astronomical model}
{wiki}

A model in which the <Earth> is stationary at the centre of the relevant celestial system. Distinguish this broad class from a particular ancient planetary arrangement.

= Tychonic system
{parent=Geocentric model}
{c}
{wiki}

A geoheliocentric arrangement with the <Sun> circling a stationary <Earth> and other planets circling the <Sun>. It can reproduce the <phases of Venus> without an orbiting <Earth>.

= Heliocentric model
{parent=Astronomy}

= Heliocentrism
{synonym}

An astronomical model in which Earth and the other planets orbit the Sun. Its acceptance and detailed mathematical form have distinct histories; heliocentrism alone does not imply <elliptic orbits>.

= Angular separation
{parent=Astronomy}
{title2=$\theta$}

The angle between two lines of sight. For a small transverse physical separation $s$ at distance $D$, $\theta\simeq s/D$ in radians. At cosmological distances $D$ is the appropriate <angular diameter distance>.

= Astrolabe
{parent=Astronomy}
{wiki}

A planispheric <astrolabe> uses a <stereographic projection> of the <celestial sphere> as a physical calculating device. A rotating star map is compared with a latitude-specific plate; a sighting rule measures an object's angular <altitude>. Aligning that measurement with the engraved geometry permits time and celestial-configuration calculations.<Astrolabe by Silke Ackermann>[Epact: components and time-telling procedure]{ref}

= Alidade
{parent=Astrolabe}
{wiki}

A sighting rule used to measure a direction or angle. On a conventional <astrolabe>, it rotates over the back's angular scale; it is distinct from the front rule used to read the projected sky geometry.

= Line-of-sight projection
{parent=Astronomy}

The map from a three-dimensional astronomical distribution to its observed sky position or integrated surface signal. For a scalar volume emissivity $j(\mathbf x)$ in simple transparent geometry, <surface brightness> is proportional to $\int j\,dl$ along the ray. Reconstructing the spatial distribution requires additional geometry or symmetry assumptions; a projected surface distribution is not a unique three-dimensional <density>.

= Planck Collaboration
{parent=Astronomy}
{c}

Scientific collaboration responsible for analysis of the Planck mission data.

= Work by Planck Collaboration
{parent=Planck Collaboration}
{c}

= Eclipse
{parent=Astronomy}
{wiki}

= Solar eclipse
{parent=Eclipse}
{wiki}

A solar eclipse occurs when the <Moon> obscures part or all of the <Sun> as seen from a specified location. Totality allows observations of nearby background <stars> that ordinary daylight hides.

= Astronomer
{parent=Astronomy}
